    "description": "Position Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning (MEL) Officer Duty station Theday Head Office and field locations in Ethiopia (travel required) Reports to Project Manager, with technical access to CEO; main counterpart to the FRP Evidence and Learning (E&L) Lead Employment Full-time, project-funded; fixed-term subject to signed contract Works with Out-grower, Finance, HR, Sales, QA, extension agents, collection-center staff and partner field focal points Project context Theday Agro Industry PLC is implementing an inclusive fruit value-chain development project under the Mastercard Foundation Fund for Resilience and Prosperity (FRP). The project seeks to expand structured sourcing from smallholder fruit farmers, strengthen Good Agricultural Practices and post-harvest handling, facilitate market linkages, develop youth-led regional distribution and other enterprises, and create dignified and fulfilling work opportunities. It prioritizes financially disadvantaged young women and men, including persons with disabilities, refugees and internally displaced persons, while supporting climate-resilient production and value addition. Purpose of the position Design and operate the project MEL system in accordance with the signed Annex 2E, FRP requirements and the final approved project budget and work plan, produce credible, timely and auditable evidence of project outputs, inclusion and Youth in Work outcomes; and help management use findings to improve implementation. The MEL Officer is TheDay's main counterpart to the FRP Evidence and Learning team. Key duties and responsibilities Work with the FRP Evidence and Learning team to develop the project's Results Measurement and Learning (RML) Plan in the first three months of implementation, and keep it up to date. Translate the signed Annex 2E into a practical indicator reference sheet defining numerator/denominator, eligibility, units, age/sex/disability/displacement disaggregation, milestones, data sources, frequency, responsibilities and verification methods. Establish baseline and enrolment records and unique beneficiary identifiers, so that every young person supported or employed is registered once, with baseline information and consent, before their support begins; implement safeguards against double counting across direct jobs, farmer pathways, seasonal work, intercropping, distribution SMEs, extension services, waste enterprises and seedling enterprises. Develop and maintain secure digital beneficiary databases, registers and supporting evidence for recruitment, payroll, training, farmer participation, agreements, documented delivery/sales and improved income/work outcomes as relevant. Design questionnaires, field checklists, consent forms and routine reporting templates; train enumerators, extension agents and responsible staff on correct use. Coordinate field monitoring, spot checks, data-quality assessments, sampling and follow-up; validate completeness, consistency and supporting evidence before results are reported. Capture baseline information on each person's work and income at registration, using the FRP baseline questions, consistent with approved FRP definitions and ethical procedures. Monitor inclusive outreach and results for women, PWDs, refugees/IDPs and marginalized youth; analyze participation, retention, accessibility barriers and corrective measures. Track outcomes related to farmer sourcing, GAP training, organic certification preparation, post-harvest loss, youth enterprise viability and market links where specified in the approved RML Plan. Maintain dashboards, indicator progress reports, source-document registers and data-quality issue logs; flag underperformance and recommend feasible corrective actions. Work with FRP verification, follow-up surveys, and lead monitoring/audit missions, including logistics, and data provision ensuring transparent access to verified records without overstating results. Contribute accurate indicator tables and learning summaries for six-monthly FRP reporting and final reporting; support documented adaptive management and lessons learned. Facilitate a short quarterly review with the project team on what the data shows and what to adjust. Apply data protection, informed consent, safeguarding, confidential case-handling and restricted access to personally identifiable information. Ensure equal understanding among all project stakeholders including in house staff and conduct capacity building as needed. Deliverables / performance expectations Operational MEL framework, indicator reference sheets, tools and beneficiary database established early in the project, before project support begins; RML Plan agreed with FRP within the first three months. Baseline and routinely updated, disaggregated results supported by traceable source evidence. Periodic dashboard, field monitoring reports, data-quality assessments and learning briefs. Verification-ready documentation for agreed Youth in Work pathways and other Annex indicators stated in FRP agreement. Final cleaned dataset, data dictionary and evidence handover in accordance with donor and company rules.",
